# id|title|source|reading_scope|chronology|mechanism|competing_or_limited_inference|exercise|status
CASE01|1987 crash|RX16|Carlson 2007 chronology and competing SEC/CME/CFTC assessments|Pre-crash selling and stress;19October collapse; market dislocations and liquidity response.|Dynamic portfolio hedging, cash/futures linkages and liquidity constraints can reinforce pressure.|Do not attribute the entire event to portfolio insurance; contemporary inquiries differed and event data do not isolate one cause.|Reconstruct a hedge selling into falling prices with finite liquidity; compare a precommitted static protection alternative and its cost.|bounded_evidence_pack
CASE02|LTCM1998|R67|Full Parkinson 6May 1999 testimony|1998 market stress; leveraged positions and counterparty concern;1999 review of leverage/information/collateral practices.|Leverage and illiquid exposures interact with creditor terms and incomplete knowledge of aggregate risk.|The testimony is an official retrospective policy account, not the complete position book or proof all hedge funds shared LTCM's leverage.|With equity 100 and assets 2000, stress a 5% loss plus financing withdrawal; explain why collateral does not replace counterparty analysis.|bounded_evidence_pack
CASE03|2008 AIG and systemic crisis|RX25|FCIC chapter 19 selected pp 344–350 and chapter conclusion|Summer liquidity concerns;12September emergency discussions;15September downgrades/collateral demands;16September financing response.|CDS collateral, short-term funding, securities-lending reinvestment and rating triggers create joint cash stress.|AIG is one mechanism within 2008, not the whole crisis. Majority report causal conclusions and broader dissents need separate comparison before a full historical lesson.|Build a dated hypothetical collateral/funding ladder; distinguish economic loss, disputed marks, liquidity and solvency without inventing AIG trade-level data.|bounded_evidence_pack
CASE04|February 2018 volatility-product disruption|RX17|BIS March 2018 pp 4–6 and graph correction|5February equity/volatility shock; leveraged/inverse product rebalancing; subsequent examination of market feedback.|Daily reset exposure changes force rebalance demand as futures move, potentially magnifying the disturbance.|A mechanism is not identification of the original shock or every participant's flows; exact product acceleration thresholds require the historical prospectus.|Simulate a daily inverse exposure after a large futures increase; track target leverage and rebalance demand, then add non-fill and market-impact scenarios.|bounded_evidence_pack
CASE05|COVID/March 2020 and WTI|RX18 R42|FSB report landing/overview discovered; detailed attachment timed out. CFTC interim-report landing examined.|March dash for liquidity and margin stress;20April WTI contract disturbance; official retrospective/interim reports.|Funding demand, liquidity withdrawal and product-specific delivery constraints are questions to reconstruct, not interchangeable explanations.|Detailed FSB sections and WTI report full text remain unreviewed; causal or numerical claims excluded. Pandemic equity stress and WTI expiry must be separate subcases.|Create a synthetic shock with simultaneous margin increase and liquidity loss; identify which historical primary section would confirm each proposed mechanism.|bounded_research_only
CASE06|Meme-stock volatility 2021|R68|SEC staff report pp 24–34 and GME options discussion|January price/volume surge; clearing collateral pressure and broker restrictions;October staff report.|Short covering, customer demand, settlement/capital constraints and options activity need separate evidence.|SEC staff found no gamma-squeeze evidence in its January GME analysis; this is not proof such mechanisms never occur or a universal explanation of every meme stock.|Compare three hypotheses using a claims/evidence table; show why OI alone cannot identify dealer hedges. No invented exact dealer positions.|bounded_evidence_pack
CASE07|0DTE growth and short-volatility failure|R32 RX17|Cboe 2May 2025 executive summary; linked full report not reviewed; BIS2018 mechanism|Expansion of short-dated use; dated exchange summary; comparison with a documented daily-reset failure mechanism.|Near-expiry convexity and liquidity interact with exposure sizing; product reset risk differs from a listed-option position.|Exchange volume/positioning estimates have sample and classification limitations. No universal claim that 0DTE stabilizes or destabilizes markets; growth does not establish safety or edge.|PR12 sign scenarios plus a near-expiry gap; distinguish terminal limited loss from funding, package execution and market-wide impact.|bounded_evidence_pack
